//@version=5
// Fibonacci pivots + 9 EMA. NIFTY.
//
// Levels from the PREVIOUS session: P = (H+L+C)/3, then 0.382 / 0.618 / 1.000
// of the prior range.
//
// Swept over 44 combinations on NIFTY hourly, 722 sessions
// (PIVOT_EMA9_NIFTY.md). The verdict is that no combination survives, and the
// reason is worth knowing before using this:
//
//   * the best-RANKED cell (R2/S2 bounce against the EMA, RR 2) returns
//     +0.057 R -- and sits at the 0th percentile of its shuffle control,
//     which returns +0.245. Shuffling a session destroys trend, and a bounce
//     is a bet against trend, so mean reversion looks BETTER on scrambled
//     data. The profit is the geometry of a 2R bet, not the pivot level.
//   * it also fails the second half of the sample (-0.087).
//   * the only cell that beats its shuffle control is P bounce WITH the EMA
//     (100th percentile, positive in both halves) -- and it is -0.186 on
//     BANKNIFTY.
//
// What held across the whole grid, rather than in one cell:
//   * BOUNCE and BREAK want OPPOSITE EMA conditions. At R1/S1 a bounce is
//     +0.012 counter-EMA and -0.152 aligned. Mean reversion wants price
//     extended AGAINST the average.
//   * P is a trend level, R1/R2 are reversion levels: at P the ordering flips.
//   * R3/S3 does nothing -- all eight cells negative.
//   * the next pivot is too close to use as a target; RR 2 beat it in almost
//     every row.
indicator("Fib Pivots + 9 EMA — NIFTY", overlay=true)

emaLen = input.int(9, "EMA length", minval=2, group="EMA",
   tooltip="On gold, EMA 5 and EMA 9 give IDENTICAL results for pivot BREAK setups -- price sits on the same side of both averages on 89.6% of bars and a break is always in that 90%. The length only matters for BOUNCE setups, where EMA 5 was better (+0.102 vs -0.041 on h1 R1/S1).")
showP  = input.bool(true,  "P",      group="Levels")
show1  = input.bool(true,  "R1/S1",  group="Levels")
show2  = input.bool(true,  "R2/S2",  group="Levels")
show3  = input.bool(false, "R3/S3",  group="Levels",
   tooltip="All eight R3/S3 cells were negative. Off by default.")

ph = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "D", high[1],  lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_on)
pl = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "D", low[1],   lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_on)
pc = request.security(syminfo.tickerid, "D", close[1], lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_on)
// lookahead is safe: [1] refers to YESTERDAY's completed daily bar

p   = (ph + pl + pc) / 3.0
rng = ph - pl
r1 = p + 0.382 * rng, s1 = p - 0.382 * rng
r2 = p + 0.618 * rng, s2 = p - 0.618 * rng
r3 = p + 1.000 * rng, s3 = p - 1.000 * rng

e = ta.ema(close, emaLen)

plot(e, "9 EMA", color.new(color.orange, 0), 2)
plot(showP ? p  : na, "P",  color.new(color.yellow, 0), 2, plot.style_linebr)
plot(show1 ? r1 : na, "R1", color.new(color.red, 20),   1, plot.style_linebr)
plot(show1 ? s1 : na, "S1", color.new(color.teal, 20),  1, plot.style_linebr)
plot(show2 ? r2 : na, "R2", color.new(color.red, 40),   1, plot.style_linebr)
plot(show2 ? s2 : na, "S2", color.new(color.teal, 40),  1, plot.style_linebr)
plot(show3 ? r3 : na, "R3", color.new(color.red, 60),   1, plot.style_linebr)
plot(show3 ? s3 : na, "S3", color.new(color.teal, 60),  1, plot.style_linebr)

// price extended AGAINST the 9 EMA at an outer level is the bounce condition
// the grid favoured; aligned is the one it favoured at P.
atR2 = show2 and high >= r2 and close < r2 and close < e
atS2 = show2 and low  <= s2 and close > s2 and close > e
plotshape(atR2, "R2 bounce (counter-EMA)", shape.triangledown, location.abovebar,
          color.new(color.red, 0), size=size.tiny)
plotshape(atS2, "S2 bounce (counter-EMA)", shape.triangleup, location.belowbar,
          color.new(color.teal, 0), size=size.tiny)
